  "image_name": "0c4ac254f749d26738f0c4b5b81545ff.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how female sex hormones may shape immune pathways that drive or limit joint inflammation, likely in an autoimmune setting. At the center is a myeloid dendritic cell/macrophage, which both interacts with antibodies and produces inflammatory mediators such as TNF-α, IL-6, IL-1β, and ROS, leading to joint inflammation and damage.\n\nThe figure suggests that progesterone and oestrogen suppress several proinflammatory steps. Both hormones appear to inhibit IL-12 and IFN-γ signaling between antigen-presenting cells and T helper 1 cells, and they also inhibit inflammatory mediator release from the myeloid cell. A regulatory T cell is shown restraining both T helper 1 responses and the myeloid cell, with arrows from the hormones toward the Treg cell implying that these hormones may promote this regulatory arm. A T helper 17 cell contributes to inflammation through IL-17, although one hormone-related effect here is marked with a question mark, indicating uncertainty.\n\nOn the right, B cells produce IgG antibodies, including a form with terminal galactose. These antibodies engage activating or inhibitory Fc receptors on the myeloid cell, influencing inflammation. Hormonal effects on B-cell antibody production or glycosylation are also indicated but marked as uncertain.",
